Specifically:

  Sources/MusterMac/KeyTranslation.swift ports the NSEvent-to-key-event heuristics
  from macos/Sources/Ghostty/NSEvent+Extension.swift - which modifiers a keyboard
  layout consumed, how to read an unshifted codepoint, and which characters must
  not reach a terminal as text.

  Sources/MusterMac/FindBar.swift ports the find bar from
  macos/Sources/Ghostty/Surface View/SurfaceView.swift (SurfaceSearchOverlay) and
  macos/Sources/Helpers/Backport.swift (BackportSelectionTextField) - the layout,
  the corner-snapping drag, the button style, and what escape and return do. What
  it counts is Muster's own: the search runs in Muster's core against history held
  by a session daemon, not in the renderer.

  Sources/MusterCore/Input/Key.generated.swift and
  Sources/MusterVT/KeyMapping.generated.swift are generated from Ghostty's
  src/input/keycodes.zig, itself derived from Chromium's DOM code table
  (BSD-style license).

Muster also links libghostty and libghostty-vt from the same project. Those are
built from source at the commit pinned in deps/ghostty.pin and are not vendored
here.

libghostty brings other projects with it. It is built from a Zig package set,
and the static archive linked into Contents/MacOS/muster carries the object code
of ten of them: gettext, breakpad, sentry-native, FreeType, libpng, zlib,
oniguruma, Wuffs, SPIRV-Cross and glslang. licenses/GHOSTTY-EMBEDDED.md names
each with its license and reproduces the text, and ships in the bundle as
Contents/Resources/GHOSTTY-EMBEDDED.md. Which ten is measured rather than read
off a manifest - a static archive contributes only the members the linker pulls,
and on macOS that is a narrower set than the package list suggests.
libghostty-vt.dylib carries none of them.

One of those is copyleft and worth stating plainly. What links in from gettext
is gettext-runtime/intl, under the LGPL, version 2.1 or later - not the GPL half
of that package, which is the tools and does not ship. Static linking under the
LGPL asks that whoever holds the binary be able to relink it against their own
libintl. Muster can offer that rather than promise it: this source is public and
Apache-2.0, deps/ghostty.pin names the commit libghostty was built from, and
that build resolves gettext at a pinned hash, so ./dev rebuilds the whole chain
from source with whatever libintl you put in it.

None of this reaches the network. sentry-native and breakpad are linked in and
are what Ghostty uses to write a crash report, and Ghostty compiles no DSN into
them - the reports are written to disk for a person to send on if they choose.
Muster changes nothing about that and adds no endpoint of its own.

Muster's app bundle redistributes herdr (https://github.com/herdrdev/herdr),
licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0. The binary is the release named
in deps/herdr.pin, unmodified; a copy of its license ships beside it in the
bundle and is kept at licenses/herdr/LICENSE. That copy is herdr's own file
byte for byte, including the unfilled placeholders in the Apache appendix -
which is why it names nobody, and why this paragraph rather than that file is
where herdr is attributed. Muster starts that daemon and speaks to it over its
socket, and contains no herdr source.

Muster links swift-protobuf (https://github.com/apple/swift-protobuf), licensed
under the Apache License, Version 2.0, at the version pinned in
Package.resolved. Its runtime library is compiled into Contents/MacOS/muster
rather than only generating code at build time, so it is redistributed.

Muster also links around sixty Rust crates into libmuster.dylib, muster-cli and
muster-bridge. They are compiled in rather than copied beside them, so nothing
in the bundle's own contents shows that they are there; licenses/THIRD-PARTY.md
names every one and reproduces the license text it published, and ships in the
bundle as Contents/Resources/THIRD-PARTY.md. tools/licenses.py writes that file
from the dependency graph, and ./dev fails if it has stopped describing what the
binaries actually contain.

Two of those crates ask for more than a copy of their license. prost is
Apache-2.0 only rather than the usual dual offer, so its terms travel with the
binary on Apache-2.0's own conditions. unicode-ident is
"(MIT OR Apache-2.0) AND Unicode-3.0" - the AND is not a choice, and the
Unicode terms apply on top of whichever of the other two you take. Both texts
are in licenses/THIRD-PARTY.md.

